# Coach speech (Latvian): the building blocks every line uses (src/coach/catalog.cpp renders them).
# Same keys as assets/coach/speech/en/common.lang, plus the grammatical forms Latvian needs.
# Register: the coach says "tu" to the player ("tavs", "tev", imperatives "paņem", "skaties"); the
# game menus say "jūs". The coach is male ("esmu priecīgs", "esmu redzējis"). The player's gender is
# unknown: Latvian finite verbs have no gender ("tu paņēmi", "tu uzvarēsi"), but participles and
# adjectives do, so never "tu esi uzvarējis", "esi gatavs", "esi uzmanīgs" about the player: a finite
# verb, an adverb ("Uzmanīgi!") or a noun instead. Words: glossary/lv.md of the translation brief.

# ---- Pieces --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nouns (Latvian Chess Federation): bandinieks, zirgs, laidnis, tornis, karalis (masculine), dāma
# (feminine). Owners: your = "tavs", my = "mans" (they agree with the noun); in the Analysis mode
# white = "balto", black = "melno", the genitive plural of "baltie" / "melnie" (White, Black):
# "balto zirgs" = White's knight, "melno dāmu" = Black's queen (acc); only the noun changes.
# EVERY form below is defined for all six pieces and all four owners (your, my, white, black), so
# {your:X}, {my:X}, {your2:X}, {my2:X}, {t1:X}, {t2:X} and {piece:X} all work. Instrumental: Latvian
# says "ar" + accusative ("ar {my:acc}"), there is no "ins" form. Cases:
#   nom        tavs zirgs / tava dāma       subject, and the object of a debitive whose agent is not
#                                           the owner: "{my} uzbrūk {your:dat}.", "Tev jāpaņem {my}."
#   gen        tava zirga / tavas dāmas     "of", and after aiz, bez, no, pēc, pie, pirms, (...) dēļ:
#                                           "{your:gen} drošība", "Aiz {my:gen} stāv {t1}."
#   dat        tavam zirgam / tavai dāmai   after uzbrukt (attack), draudēt (threaten), līdz, blakus
#                                           (next to), priekšā (in front of), and the owner with "būt":
#                                           "{your:dat} bija labāks gājiens.", "{t1:dat} priekšā"
#   acc        tavu zirgu / tavu dāmu       direct object; after ar, uz, par, pret, caur, pa, ap, gar:
#                                           "Es paņemu {your:acc}.", "Šahs ar {my:acc}!"
#   loc        tavā zirgā / tavā dāmā       (rare) "Visa problēma ir {your:loc}."
# The reflexive possessive "savs" REPLACES tavs / mans / balto / melno when the owner is the subject
# of the clause: the "tu" of an imperative or of a 2nd person verb, the coach's "es", the dative
# agent of a debitive ("tev jā..."), "baltie" / "melnie". "Paskaties uz {your:refl_acc}" (never
# "uz tavu ..."), "Es pārvietoju {my:refl_acc} uz {sq}" (never "manu ...").
#   refl_nom   savs zirgs / sava dāma       object of a debitive: "Tev jāpaglābj {your:refl_nom}."
#   refl_gen   sava zirga / savas dāmas     "Padomā par {your:refl_gen} drošību."
#   refl_dat   savam zirgam / savai dāmai   "Atrodi {your:refl_dat} drošāku lauciņu."
#   refl_acc   savu zirgu / savu dāmu       "Tu divreiz pārvietoji {your:refl_acc}."
#   refl_loc   savā zirgā / savā dāmā       (rare, defined for completeness)
# The noun alone, when the owner is clear or an adjective stands before the noun:
#   bare_nom   zirgs / dāma                 "Piesiet{your:def} {your:bare_nom} nevar aizsargāt {sq}."
#   bare_gen   zirga / dāmas                "{my:bare_gen} gājiens"
#   bare_dat   zirgam / dāmai               "Uzbrūc {my:bare_dat}!"
#   bare_acc   zirgu / dāmu                 "Ņem ar {your:bare_acc}!"
#   bare_loc   zirgā / dāmā                 (rare) "Bandinieks pārvēršas {my:bare_loc}."
# Pronouns for the piece just named ("tas / tā", as Latvian says of things; never "viņš / viņa"):
#   pron       tas / tā                     "Lūk, {your}. {your:pron} iet pa diagonāli."
#   pron_gen   tā / tās                     "aiz {your:pron_gen}" (behind it); also "its": "{my:pron_gen}
#                                           lauciņš" (when the piece is not the subject; else "savs")
#   pron_dat   tam / tai                    "Es uzbrūku {your:pron_dat}."
#   pron_acc   to / to                      "Paņem {my:pron_acc}!", "ar {my:pron_acc}"
#   pron_loc   tajā / tajā                  (rare)
#   this       šis / šī                     "{your:this} ir {your}." = "Šis ir tavs tornis." / "Šī ir
#                                           tava dāma." (or say "Lūk, {your}." with no agreement)
# Endings that agree with the piece, glued to a stem right after it (nominative singular only; in
# another case, or for two pieces at once, rephrase: "uzbrukums gan {t1:dat}, gan {t2:dat}"):
#   adj        s / a      an indefinite adjective or passive participle whose masculine ends in -s:
#                         "{your} ir piesiet{your:adj}." = piesiets / piesieta; likewise neaizsargāt-,
#                         ieslēgt-, apdraudēt-, zaudēt-, droš-, brīv-, vērt- ("{your} ir vērt{your:adj}
#                         {n:point_acc}"). Not for the adjectives in -š (vājš / vāja).
#   def        ais / ā    the definite form before a bare noun: "piesiet{your:def} {your:bare_nom}" =
#                         piesietais zirgs / piesietā dāma.
#   is         is / usi   a past active participle whose two forms share their stem: "{your} ir
#                         iesprūd{your:is}" = iesprūdis / iesprūdusi (also nokļuv-, pazud-, aizgāj-; NOT
#                         palicis / palikusi). A finite past is simpler: "{your} iesprūda".
